00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.430
we need to take a brief
detour through the analog era.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.140
Because while old-timey rich men

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:21.060
didn't know the difference

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.400
between a Dogecoin and a Bennifer,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.310
they did know the power of
owning the means of cultural

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:27.680
and journalistic production.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.410
For more on this, see the quaint
indie film, "Citizen Kane."

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.190
Dudes like William Randolph Hearst,

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:34.870
the inspiration for "Citizen Kane,"

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.870
used the newspapers they owned
to push their own agendas.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:38.970
This allowed them to do things

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.160
like garner national support for a war,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.180
Hearst was partial to the
Spanish-American variety,

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.950
or simply to seek revenge
against their industry rivals.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.600
For the ultra-rich of
the early 20th century,

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.340
owning a newspaper was basically

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.710
like having a personal megaphone.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.600
Eventually, feeling like their voices

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.460
still weren't quite loud enough,

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.250
the Hearsts of the world
began acquiring entire chains

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.770
of newspapers to further
propagate their beliefs.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:05.290
But in the post-World War II era,

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:07.610
things like journalistic integrity

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.630
and editorial independence became trendy,

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.180
and the idea of a rich old dude

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.760
that lived in a castle
owning all the newspapers

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.080
started to seem a bit gauche.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.593
And American journalism lived
happily ever after, right?

00:05:21.593 --> 00:05:22.426
Right?

00:05:22.426 --> 00:05:24.810
It did, it lived happily ever after?

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.430
Yeah, not really.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.670
To say nothing about the
fleecing of American newsrooms,

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:30.730
pivots to video,

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:34.090
and the push from news to
content in the past decades,

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:35.990
we are once again living through an era

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.620
of super-rich people owning media outlets.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.900
And also, an era of really good TV shows

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.290
about super-rich dudes
owning media outlets.

