00:07:05.087 --> 00:07:06.800
(dramatic music)

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.880
- So, as you can see,
Musk's bid for Twitter

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.450
is far from the first time

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.100
that a rich one-percenter
purchased a media company,

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.470
maybe for the purpose of
directing media narratives

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:18.640
or making sure people can't
say mean things about them.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:20.450
And to put my cards on the table,

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:23.760
the only truly mean thing I
have to say about Elon Musk

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.510
is that men who get hair
transplants are sellouts

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:27.580
and there is no room

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.080
for self-hating bald men in my circle.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.627
Now, you might be thinking,

00:07:31.627 --> 00:07:34.140
"Elon isn't like the
other billionaire boys,

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:37.280
because he's not buying a
newspaper or cable news network

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.550
to reinforce his own ideological agenda,

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.300
he's just purchasing a
demented global town hall,

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.340
where he will sit back
and let everyone express

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:47.840
whatever views they want,
280 characters at a time."

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.950
And while we get where you're
coming from, in another sense,

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:53.430
the fact that Musk isn't
simply buying a paper

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:56.200
or media network makes
things even more extreme.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.820
That's because he's not
just buying a media source,

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.180
he's buying the place where
a majority of news media

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.400
and information is posted and distributed.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:05.980
In other words, he might
not be buying a newspaper,

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:08.910
but he's purchasing the
proverbial digital printing press

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.410
and distribution networks used

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:12.450
by every news and media company,

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.740
along with basically every
writer and journalist,

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:15.610
on the planet.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:16.540
And in doing so,

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:19.250
he's going beyond the trend
of members of the ruling class

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.410
owning media companies as pet projects.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:23.210
And to understand what this means,

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:26.450
we need to talk about something
called platform capitalism.

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:27.800
To understand what it is,

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.580
let's start with the
first part, platforms.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.950
While they are sometimes
places you can stand

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:34.010
while you wait to catch a train

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.770
to a magical school of wizardry,

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:38.880
these days platforms usually
refers to apps and websites

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.040
like Uber, AirBnb, and Facebook.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:43.217
As the book, "Platform
Revolution" puts it,

00:08:43.217 --> 00:08:45.060
"A platform is a business based

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.350
on enabling value-creating interactions

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:49.430
between external producers and consumers,"

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:52.310
providing "an open,
participative infrastructure

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:53.540
for these interactions"

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:55.750
and setting "governance
conditions for them."

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:58.127
Or, as theorist and academic
Nick Srnicek puts it,

00:08:58.127 --> 00:08:59.340
"What are platforms?

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:00.400
At the most general level,

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:02.100
platforms are digital infrastructures

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.260
that enable two or more
groups to interact.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.780
They therefore position
themselves as intermediaries

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.380
that bring together different users:

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.530
customers, advertisers, service providers,

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:13.180
producers, suppliers, and
even physical objects."

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:14.800
And unlike, say, a newspaper,

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.720
which is a physical product
that can be sold for profit,

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:19.830
platforms don't really
create anything of value,

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.190
at least not in the traditional sense.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:24.790
According to Srnicek, "By
providing a digital space

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:26.360
for others to interact in,

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.920
platforms position themselves
so as to extract data

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.430
from natural processes,

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:32.500
weather conditions,
crop cycles, et cetera,

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.010
from production processes,

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.900
assembly lines, continuous
flow manufacturing, et cetera,

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:39.120
and from other businesses and users,

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.510
web tracking, usage data, et cetera.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.630
They are an extractive
apparatus for data."

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.280
So while old-school billionaires

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:47.570
might've used their media companies

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:50.700
to push their own views and
agendas onto media consumers,

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.640
new-school billionaires like
Musk use platforms like Twitter

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.300
to extract data from those of
us who use these platforms.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:00.070
In this way, we can see
Musk's acquisition of Twitter

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:02.590
as evidence of what writer
and professor Jill Lapore

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:06.040
refers to as Muskism,
or, extreme capitalism.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.880
As Lepore sees it, a childhood of reading,

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:11.080
and, according to a recent
video on this channel,

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:12.878
misreading science fiction

00:10:12.878 --> 00:10:15.570
has led Musk to favor a sort of capitalism

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.380
which is more theoretical than industrial.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:19.213
As she describes it,

00:10:19.213 --> 00:10:21.300
"I think it is an extreme
form of capitalism

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:22.810
in the sense that it's plutocratic.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:24.430
It's something old again.

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:26.980
There's a lot of feudalism in Muskism.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:28.520
It's like there are these lords

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:30.350
and the rest of us are the peasantry

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:33.540
and our fates are in their
hands because they know best."

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.840
And the idea that Musk's
brand of capitalism

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:38.680
is sending us back towards
Feudalism caught on quickly

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.740
after he announced his
purchase of Twitter,

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.570
with people expressing concern
that, with Musk in charge,

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:46.840
every time we tweet we'll
actually be doing free labor

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.520
on Musk's digital land.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.240
But Srnicek questions
the argument that posting

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:53.110
is equivalent to doing unpaid labor.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:55.950
He says that, "Free
labor is only a portion

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:57.490
of the multitude of data sources

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.530
that a company like Google relies upon:

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:00.650
economic transactions,

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:03.590
information collected by sensors
in the internet of things,

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:05.040
corporate and government data,

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.150
such as credit records
and financial records,

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.050
and public and private surveillance,

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:11.820
such as the cars used to
build up Google Maps."

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.290
So even when we're not actively posting,

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:16.470
if that's something you're capable of,

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.180
we're still creating
data simply by existing,

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:22.400
or being alive in, our
largely digital economy.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:25.070
We're creating data
basically all the time.

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:27.240
Srnicek goes on to
further disconnect posting

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:28.357
from traditional labor,

00:11:28.357 --> 00:11:29.910
"Beyond the intuitive hesitation

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:31.920
to think that messaging friends is labor,

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.450
any idea of socially necessary labor time,

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:37.000
the implicit standard against
which production processes

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.560
are set, is lacking.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:40.910
This means there are no
competitive pressures

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:42.550
for getting users to do more,

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:45.670
even if there are pressures to
get them to do more online."

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:47.680
While a feudal laborer
couldn't really choose

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.240
to not work their land,

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.800
we can choose to put our
phones away and not post.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.190
So the good news seems to be

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.100
that when you tweet at
your favorite director

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.110
giving them your notes
on their latest release,

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:00.600
you're not technically
doing free labor for Musk.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.000
The bad news is that no one is forcing you

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.630
to tweet other than yourself.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:08.670
But labor or not, he's collecting
a fuck-ton of your data.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:10.850
Like, you know, that
you're watching this video.

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:12.870
Right now. In your house.

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.640
But let's get back to
the negative reactions

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.420
to Musk's Twitter acquisition.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.790
Much of the anger seemed
to be based on the idea

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:20.660
that people enjoy using Twitter,

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:22.660
but they don't enjoy
the person of Elon Musk,

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:24.740
and thus, Musk's acquisition of Twitter

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:26.940
means they will now enjoy it less.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:29.240
Sort of like if some dude who
bullied you in high school

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:30.630
bought your favorite sandwich shop,

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:34.150
and now every time you bite
into that turkey pesto sub,

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:35.730
you can almost taste the toilet water

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.010
he used to dunk your head in.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:38.810
For others, it seems
that they worry that Musk

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:41.310
will somehow impose his
personal political views,

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:43.730
whatever they are, onto
the platform in some way.

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:45.540
And this isn't an unfounded worry,

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:48.030
as according to Srnicek,
"Platforms are also designed

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.690
in a way that makes them
attractive to its varied users.

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:53.190
While often presenting
themselves as empty spaces

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:54.560
for others to interact on,

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.260
they in fact embody a politics.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:58.850
The rules of product
and service development,

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:00.600
as well as marketplace interactions,

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.270
are set by the platform owner."

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:04.420
Though, as of the writing of this script,

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:05.910
the main changes he's discussed

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:08.730
are charging brands and larger
accounts for quote tweets,

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:10.930
potentially monetizing viral tweets,

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:13.090
as well as the addition of an edit button.

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:15.810
But for many it simply seems
to be the case that Elon Musk

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:17.290
owning a popular platform

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.620
is crossing some type of moral boundary.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.430
But that worry also seems
to imply that up until now,

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:24.644
Twitter was owned by the good guys.

00:13:24.644 --> 00:13:26.340
And who are those good guys?

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:27.930
Well until Musk takes over,

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:29.520
the majority shareholders of Twitter

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:31.300
are Morgan Stanley Investment Management,

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:33.270
The Black Rock group, Vanguard Funds,

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:34.740
and some Saudi investors.

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.310
None of these dudes have ever been accused

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.040
of being too woke.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:41.140
And Black Rock was at one point
the world's largest investor

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:42.480
in coal plant developers,

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.550
and is currently one of the
world's largest investors

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.040
in weapons manufacturing.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.350
I mean, say what you will about Elon,

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:50.830
but at least the dude is more
into batteries than coal.

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:53.360
And rockets that go into
space more than rockets

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:56.020
that accidentally hit
hospitals full of civilians.

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:58.470
So what makes Elon Musk any better

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:00.230
or worse than those companies?

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:03.640
Or, for that matter, the
owners of Meta, Google, Amazon,

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:06.340
or any number of prominent
digital platforms?

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:09.250
Some of the outcry seems to
come from a genuine desire

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:12.290
for a version of what we
could call ethical capitalism,

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:13.980
or ethical consumerism,

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:16.020
where our money, and,
in the case of Twitter,

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:17.280
our time and attention,

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:18.990
is spent supporting brands and companies

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:20.610
that share our values.

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:23.060
This mentality has been on
the rise in recent years,

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:25.850
and is particularly prominent
amongst millennials.

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:27.210
There are even financial funds

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:29.500
that specialize in
investing money in companies

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.300
deemed to be adequately ethical

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:33.070
and/or environmentally friendly.

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:34.270
This attitude looks like someone

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:35.810
who won't eat at Chick Fil A

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:37.140
because their owners have a history

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:39.910
of funding anti-LGBTQ organizations,

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:42.490
but who will eat at Chipotle
because of their commitment

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:45.090
to sourcing sustainable
and organic ingredients.

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:47.640
But just like you can't
bang your way to virginity,

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:50.110
some would argue that you
can't capitalism your way

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:51.540
to an ethical society.

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:52.537
It seems like the phrase,

00:14:52.537 --> 00:14:55.170
"There is no ethical
consumption under capitalism"

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:58.170
is becoming a crucial
proverb for our times.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:14:59.950
The idea here is that if we apply

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:01.810
the standards of individual ethics

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.330
and morality to capitalism,

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.410
which functions according to economic,

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:08.600
and not moral logic, we'll
be consistently let down.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:09.980
Much like the recent controversy

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:11.960
around Joe Rogan's deal with Spotify,

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:13.740
which similarly led users to proclaim

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:14.650
that they were leaving

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:16.570
for other digital streaming platforms,

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:19.150
we see these contradictions
when people want brands

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:21.150
to share their ethics and politics.

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:23.520
And it's not hard to see
the contradictions at play

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:26.370
when companies attempt to
convince consumers that they are,

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.430
in fact, doing ethical stuff.

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:30.390
For example, recently Blue Bottle Coffee

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:32.550
started piloting a shift to make oat milk

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:33.440
their default milk,

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.080
for the purposes of reducing
greenhouse gas emissions

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:37.490
that come from dairy production.

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:38.910
Which seems great.

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:40.720
Until you realize that
Blue Bottle is owned

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:42.100
by the Nestle corporation,

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:45.340
who, along with being a
generally very, very bad company,

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:47.260
they literally did child slavery,

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:49.260
also has a full-on dairy division.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:51.930
So even if you feel good
about your oat milk latte,

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:54.920
you're also giving money to
a company that was sued for,

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:58.380
and I can't repeat this
enough, doing child slavery.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:00.460
- It's not handmade in USA,

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:03.000
it's pronounced "hahnd-made in Oosa."

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.070
The Hand people are a
Vietnamese slave tribe,

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:08.080
and USA is their island prison.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:10.090
They made your jeans.

00:16:10.090 --> 00:16:11.943
Do you know how they get
the stitching so small?

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:13.743
Orphans.

00:16:13.743 --> 00:16:15.860
- On the other hand, there
are those who might say

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:18.300
it's unethical to eat
things like fast food,

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:19.820
due to things like food quality,

00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:22.080
environmental impact,
and labor conditions.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.180
And that it's bad to eat factory farmed

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:25.580
and non-organic meat and produce,

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:28.480
again, for environmental
impact and working conditions.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:31.740
But what does that mean for
people who can't afford organic,

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:33.090
grass-fed chicken breasts

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.270
or gluten-free cashew mac and cheese?

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:37.330
Are they to be held morally responsible

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:38.810
for their consumer choices?

00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:41.610
Or, are they simply doing
whatever they need to do

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:43.010
to survive in a system

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:45.090
that isn't providing a
lot of other options?

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:47.240
If you can afford to patronize companies

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:50.030
that treat the planet and
workers well, that's great.

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:53.010
But if you can't, you shouldn't
be held morally responsible

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:55.410
for systemic issues that
you have no control over.

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:56.830
Which brings us back to the question

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.520
of whether or not Elon Musk
is going to ruin Twitter,

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:01.930
and if leaving the platform
is a useful response

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:03.570
for folks who don't dig

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:05.410
Elon's whole libertarian space pirate

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:08.120
who is also an online
psychopharmacologist vibe.

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:11.710
So, if people decide to leave
Twitter, where will they go?

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:13.630
If you're able to simply abstract yourself

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:15.140
from social media altogether,

00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:18.560
we have nothing but the deepest
love and respect for you.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:19.830
But for those who are dependent,

00:17:19.830 --> 00:17:21.320
either socially or professionally,

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:22.900
on social media platforms,

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:25.370
where might you go if the worst guest host

00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:27.740
in the history of SNL
succeeds in buying Twitter?

00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:30.160
Maybe you head over to
Instagram or Facebook?

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:31.760
Well, they're both owned by Meta

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:33.620
which is the brainchild of this guy,

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:34.560
and if you find him to be

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:37.160
a significantly better person
than Musk, there you go.

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:37.993
But maybe you're not

00:17:37.993 --> 00:17:40.690
into Zuck's misinformation-happy
platform either,

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:44.140
so you're gonna park your
digital self firmly on TikTok?

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:46.960
Oops, they're owned by
Chinese company ByteDance,

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:48.270
which has been accused of censoring

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:50.670
and surveilling content on
behalf of the Chinese government,

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.160
which is not the best.

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:55.870
So maybe you go with a
platform not connected

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:57.100
to global corporations

00:17:57.100 --> 00:18:00.020
and find some new friends on
Craigslist missed connections.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.600
What are you trying to
do, get yourself killed?!

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:05.880
The point is, it's pretty hard
to fully disconnect ourselves

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.590
from companies that
vary from kinda sketchy

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:10.980
to literally doing human rights abuses,

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:13.760
because platforms of all
types have become inextricable

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:14.860
from modern life.

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:17.700
So unless you want to
go fully off the grid,

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:18.930
you're going to have to navigate

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:21.030
some type of relationship to platforms

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:23.470
owned by people with
names like Mark, Jeffrey,

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:25.496
and yes, even Elon.

00:18:25.496 --> 00:18:28.310
And we're not saying it's good
that Musk is buying Twitter,

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:30.530
but we are saying that moral categories

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:32.010
like good and bad

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.640
aren't the most useful
metrics to apply to platforms

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:37.450
whose sole purpose is
the extraction of data

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:38.690
for the sake of profit.

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:40.400
Asking a platform to be moral

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:42.970
is like asking a couch
cushion to be delicious.

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:45.600
Musk's reign as CEO of
Twitter might be bad,

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:48.780
it might be good, it will
almost definitely be weird.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:50.570
But one thing we do know for sure

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:52.570
is that he isn't the
only super-rich person

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:54.550
who sees value in owning a platform

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:57.850
whose sole purpose is the
collection of monetizable data.

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:00.000
And as more of our social
and professional lives

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.460
get inextricably linked
with these platforms,

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:03.770
we're all kind of stuck here,

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:06.380
whether or not we continue
sharing our flaming hot takes

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:09.180
on Twitter every time we
drink too much caffeine.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:10.520
But what do you all think?

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.260
Is Elon going to destroy the
peaceful Twitter community

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:14.930
we've come to know and love?

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:17.790
Or is the reaction to
his recent bid overblown?

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:19.344
Let us know in the comments.

00:19:19.344 --> 00:19:20.900
Hit that subscribe button

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.280
like you're launching
your high school bully

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:24.680
into deep space on a SpaceX rocket,

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:26.460
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.600
Thanks to our patrons
for all your support,

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:30.860
and be sure to check out
our new Patreon perks.

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:32.940
And, as always, thanks for watching.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:33.773
Later.

00:19:33.773 --> 00:19:36.338
(upbeat music)

